Give the legacy RandomState build of distributions.c its own symbol namespace.

numpy compiles distributions.c twice: once with RAND_INT_TYPE as int64_t for
Generator, and once with NP_RANDOM_LEGACY so RAND_INT_TYPE is long, for
RandomState, whose C API predates int64_t. distributions.h says as much: "the
original unmodified copy must be retained for use in RandomState".

Shipped as separate shared objects the two builds never meet. Linked statically
into one interpreter they do, and they are not interchangeable -- on wasm32 a
long is 32 bits and an int64_t is 64, so random_multinomial resolves to whichever
copy the linker saw first and the other module's calls become a signature
mismatch that traps at runtime. wasm-ld reports that as a warning, not an error.

The forced-include header renames the legacy build's functions, so mtrand is
self-contained and every other module keeps the canonical int64_t copy from
libnpyrandom.a. build-python.sh generates the header from distributions.h, so
the rename set follows the header rather than a list that can drift from it.

--- a/numpy/random/meson.build
+++ b/numpy/random/meson.build
@@ -76,7 +76,9 @@
       'src/distributions/distributions.c',
       'src/legacy/legacy-distributions.c'
     ],
-    ['-DNP_RANDOM_LEGACY=1'], [npymath_lib],
+    ['-DNP_RANDOM_LEGACY=1',
+     '-include', meson.current_source_dir() / 'src/legacy/nimbus-legacy-rename.h'],
+    [npymath_lib],
   ],
 ]
 foreach gen: random_pyx_sources
